 Recent work has increasingly coupled human and scene reconstruction. Human3R jointly predicts humans, scenes, and cameras, while UniSH combines human and scene features for metric reconstruction and human placement \citep{chen2026human3r,li2026unish}. UniCon3R introduces contact-aware feedback to correct human reconstruction, and GRAFT iteratively refines human parameters using nearby scene geometry \citep{sur2026unicon3r,ym2026graft}. These methods demonstrate the importance of scene-aware human reasoning, but metric ambiguity and interaction ambiguity remain closely entangled. Before local geometric discrepancies can reliably indicate whether a body is misplaced, the human and scene must first be expressed in a comparable metric space. Moreover, interaction constraints need not always be represented by explicit contact predictions; they can also be inferred from the spatial context surrounding the body.
 
-Based on this observation, we introduce \textbf{GroundedHuman}, a framework that uses the human body as a \emph{structured interface to the scene}. A body specifies where scene evidence should be gathered and supplies the geometric reference needed to interpret that evidence. Its metric extent anchors scene scale; its spatial configuration determines which surrounding observations can guide human placement. We encode these relations through body-conditioned queries and attention, then read out the corresponding geometric corrections. This view connects metric calibration and placement refinement through a common principle: human--scene relations provide the evidence from which a coherent reconstruction is grounded. Interaction is represented implicitly by these relations and their induced updates, without an intermediate contact-label prediction.
+Based on this observation, we introduce \textbf{GroundedHuman}, a framework that infers metric calibration and human placement from \emph{implicit interaction representations}. Our key idea is that the relationship between a body and its observed surroundings provides evidence for both the scene's metric reference and the body's placement within it. Human structure specifies where and how to query scene evidence; attention encodes the resulting associations into relation features, and geometric readouts translate these features into corrections. This connects calibration and placement through a common formulation: encode human--scene interaction, then infer the geometry it supports. Interaction is represented by these learned relations and their induced updates, without an intermediate contact-label prediction.
 
-\textbf{Body-Anchored Metric Calibration} first establishes the reference in which these relations can be compared. Human-surface depth correspondences provide an analytic scale initialization. Our \emph{Metric Alignment Query} then uses body anchors, conditioned on their current geometric discrepancies, to retrieve scene features and predict residual scale and depth corrections. Combining observations across people and frames yields a shared calibration for scene depth and camera translation. Human structure therefore serves both as a metric reference and as a means of selecting scene evidence for calibration.
+\textbf{Body-Anchored Metric Calibration} first establishes the reference in which regional relations can be compared. Human-surface depth correspondences provide an analytic scale initialization. Our \emph{Body-Anchored Scene Query} then relates geometrically conditioned body anchors to local scene features, forming a metric interaction representation. A metric readout predicts residual scale and depth corrections, and aggregation across people and frames yields a shared calibration for scene depth and camera translation. Human geometry therefore supplies both the metric prior and the conditions under which scene evidence is interpreted.
 
-\textbf{Dynamic Interaction Grounding} then reads the calibrated scene from the perspective of the current body placement. Our \emph{Placement-Conditioned Interaction Attention} gathers body-relative geometry across local and broader supports, treating self-surface and environmental observations as complementary evidence. Regional representations yield whole-body translation proposals, which are combined according to their reliability. Updating placement also changes the evidence: we reproject the body, rebuild its surrounding context, and attend again. This dynamic process refines root translation while preserving pose and shape, learning how body--scene relations should influence placement without explicitly classifying contact.
